# skill-router - Variant L (agentic corpus primitives, v2)

Use the `skill-router` corpus primitives as an AgenticRAG-style retrieval
harness. Do not call `skills route` and do not call `skills dci`. Do not read
skill bodies. Route from disabled-skill metadata only.

CLI: `<abs-path-to-skill-router>`.

## Workflow

1. Extract 3-7 distinctive query terms from the user request. Prefer
   terms that uniquely identify the task (technical nouns, named methods,
   APIs, file extensions, protocols, exact operations) and skip generic
   filler. For any concept that has more than one common phrasing, include
   both forms — different skills phrase the same concept differently in
   their `description`, so a single phrasing risks zero-recall on the
   correct skill.

2. Search metadata with OR-only `--any` terms (no `--all`). Start with the
   full term set:

   ```bash
   <abs-path-to-skill-router> skills corpus search \
     --any "<term1>" --any "<term2>" --any "<term3>" --any "<term4>" \
     --limit 30 --json
   ```

   The result reports `totalMatches`, `returned`, `truncated`, and stable
   `ref` values. Adjust only if necessary, within a small bounded budget
   (2-4 searches total):
   - if `totalMatches` is 0, replace one term with a broader synonym and
     retry;
   - if `truncated` is true or `totalMatches` > 30, add 1-2 more specific
     terms (still as `--any`, not `--all`) and retry;
   - never use `--all` unless three consecutive searches return >30 each.

3. **Always inspect the top-3 candidates** before committing — even if the
   top-1 looks obvious from the search snippet. The search response only
   shows truncated description snippets; the full `description` from
   `corpus inspect` often contains the distinguishing operation that
   distractors lack.

   ```bash
   <abs-path-to-skill-router> skills corpus inspect <ref1> <ref2> <ref3> --json
   ```

   If the shortlist has fewer than 3 candidates, inspect all of them. Skip
   inspect only if there is exactly 1 candidate in the shortlist.

4. Choose by metadata evidence from the inspect result (not the search
   snippet):
   - Prefer explicit operation coverage over nearby topic similarity.
   - Prefer a dedicated file-format/tool skill when the task is to operate
     that file format or tool.
   - Prefer a domain/method skill when the named method/domain constraint
     is central and the file/tool is only the substrate.
   - For retrieve/extract/structured-answer tasks, prefer metadata
     mentioning evidence, search, extraction, or structured output over
     broad discovery or planning.
   - If still tied, choose the metadata covering the most distinctive
     required verbs and nouns.

Return exactly one bare `skill-NNN`. Do not execute the user's task.
